WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.690
Five pupils, A, B, C, D and E, need only these meetings:

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.090
A with B, A with C, A with D, B with E, and C with E.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.350
A pupil can attend at most one meeting in a time slot.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.430
What is the smallest number of slots?

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.060
Pupil A is in three meetings.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.280
Those three cannot share a slot.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:25.360
So at least three slots are needed.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.750
Now try to manage with three.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.370
Slot one: A meets B, and C meets E.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.890
Slot two: A meets C, and B meets E.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.610
Slot three: A meets D.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:39.390
Check each slot.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.210
In slot one, the pupils are A, B, C and E, each once.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.610
In slot two, the same four, each once.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.410
In slot three, only A and D.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.210
All five meetings are scheduled with no clashes.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:55.450
A second limit agrees.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.300
There are five meetings, and a slot holds at most two of

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:02.190
them, since five pupils cannot form three pairs.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:04.970
Five meetings need at least three slots.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:07.790
Both limits say three, and three works.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.650
The busiest person gives a limit.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.710
Then build a timetable and check every slot.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.120
Rebuild this, then decide how many slots are needed if a

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.610
sixth meeting, D with E, is added.
